  "transcript": "# Tourism\n\n## Why the overnight visitor levy has been proposed\n> Government frames the levy as a devolution tool to give mayors local revenue and growth incentives; industry experts see motives but disagree on whether it’s the right economic solution.\n\nChair: We’ll begin with the proposed overnight visitor levy. Bernard, what problem is it meant to solve and will it work?\nBernard Donoghue: It’s trying to solve three things: poor local government funding, a precedent from other countries that levy tourist taxes, and a political drive to devolve more powers to mayors. It could work if done carefully.\nAlistair Handyside: I agree with those aims but stress the delivery matters: how the money is collected, spent and whether it’s fair across different kinds of destinations.\nAnthony Breach: The Government’s core argument links to devolution: the UK is very centralised, so giving mayors a visitor tax could align local incentives to grow their economies. But the levy must be designed to be pro-growth.\n\n\n## Who pays and who is harmed: cities vs rural areas\n> Big cities may absorb a levy more easily; rural and coastal areas—relying more on domestic, price-sensitive visitors—could suffer reduced demand and business viability.\n\nNigel Huddleston: Is it sensible to tax an industry to support it? If the levy goes ahead, should it be a flat fee or a percentage?\nBernard Donoghue: A percentage is fairer—progressive by price—but a flat £x is easiest to collect and would be regressive between cheaper and luxury accommodation.\nAlistair Handyside: A flat nightly fee is regressive for rural self-caterers and holiday parks. Our data shows a 1% price rise cuts demand by ~1.25% in rural areas—so businesses can’t simply pass it on.\nAnthony Breach: Elasticities matter: domestic leisure trips are more price-sensitive than business or international trips. Coastal communities could lose visitors if prices rise.\nBernard Donoghue: We’re already a high-tax tourism market—VAT at 20%—so adding a levy risks making the UK less competitive internationally.\n\n\n## Practicalities: collection, exemptions and timing\n> Collection raises many operational questions—who remits the tax, how to handle platform bookings, exemptions, and admin burden on micro-businesses—so transition time and impact assessments are vital.\n\nTom Rutland: Who should legally collect the levy when bookings go through platforms—the accommodation provider or the platform?\nBernard Donoghue: Different countries do it different ways. Treasury guidance is urgently needed on legal entitlement and technical responsibilities.\nAlistair Handyside: Small businesses face big admin: quarterly reporting, reclaiming exemptions and building systems is costly. Scotland and Wales show how complex this becomes in practice.\nTom Rutland: Would you want guests to pay on arrival?\nAlistair Handyside: No—prices need to be shown at booking (CMA rules) and remitted after stays. Pay-on-arrival would create disputes with guests.\nDr Huq: Should there be a long lead-in, minimum implementation period and review clauses?\nBernard Donoghue: Yes to all. This is complex; change needs time, review and safeguards like sunset clauses and clear impact assessments.\n\n\n## Hypothecation, governance and who should spend the money\n> Witnesses split: some want ringfenced (hypothecated) funds for tourism assets and services, others prefer unhypothecated income to force mayors to justify action and avoid vague promises.\n\nDr Huq: If a levy is introduced, how should proceeds be used to help tourists, businesses and communities?\nAnthony Breach: I oppose hard hypothecation. The levy’s main value is changing mayoral behaviour. Mayors must commit publicly to actions that improve the visitor economy.\nBernard Donoghue: I disagree. Ringfencing money for culture, heritage, nature and transport could be transformational—these services are not statutory and get cut first.\nAlistair Handyside: If money isn’t strictly defined, councils may absorb it to plug other budget gaps. We need specific guarantees and a forum where businesses help decide spend.\nDr Huq: Should London boroughs get a share?\nAnthony Breach: Primary collection by the mayor makes sense, with possible sharing to local boroughs for services that touch tourism, otherwise central areas would hoover up receipts.\nBernard Donoghue: London could raise ~£150m; used strategically it could fund major projects that one borough could not. That’s the argument for city-level spending.\n\n\n## Destination management and long-term funding\n> Structures—VisitBritain/VisitEngland/LVEPs—are largely right but underfunded; LVEPs need multi-year funding, business-ready product support, and infrastructure investment to spread visitors beyond London.\n\nJo Stevens: Do these many bodies (VisitBritain, VisitEngland, LVEPs, DMOs) have clear roles and do they work together?\nLaura Citron: The layered system is sensible. London & Partners acts as LVEP, convention bureau and growth agency. The problem is not structure but long-term funding to make it effective.\nJoss Croft: The de Bois review gave the right model—national to local—but funding is missing. Many LVEPs are tiny; 16% have no full-time staff and 22% aren’t sure of survival for five years.\nMerryn Kidd: LVEP designation helped us coordinate Somerset & Exmoor, but there’s no funding for staff. Even modest grants would let us prepare businesses for international trade.\nNigel Huddleston: What scale of funding is realistic?\nJoss Croft: VisitBritain’s budget fell in real terms since 2000. International competitors spend far more. Small public investment leverages large private returns; LVEPs know where to spend it.\nGill German: Infrastructure basics—bins, toilets, street cleaning and transport—make places attractive. Local improvements boost businesses if the public sees the benefit of any levy.\nTom Rutland: What specific infrastructure is most needed?\nMerryn Kidd: Transport and last-mile connections: Exmoor and Wells lack rail and reliable buses. Also digital coverage: poor mobile data hurts bookings and visitor experience.\nLaura Citron: For London, transport (e.g. Elizabeth line) and housing to retain workers have been transformational. Different places need tailored interventions.\nJeff Smith: Who should be accountable for delivering a national tourism strategy?\nJoss Croft: A cross-government, funded five-year growth plan with named delivery bodies, metrics and accountability. Tourism needs joined-up ministers and funding commitments.\n",
